# Code and documentation are stored and checked out with LF, whatever the
# platform. The repository grew up mixed: files written by an editor on Windows
# came out CRLF, files written by a script came out LF, and the two were
# interleaved across the same directories.
* text=auto eol=lf

# data/ is exempt, and that is the point rather than an oversight. These files
# are cited, and two of them were deposited with CRLF. Normalising would rewrite
# them for no gain, and leaving them to git's end-of-line machinery would let a
# checkout on another platform hand a reader different bytes than the ones the
# paper points at. Marked binary in this one sense: git stores and returns them
# exactly as committed.
data/** -text

# The dictionary is the exception to the exception: it is prose we maintain and
# edit, it happens to live beside the files it describes, and nothing cites its
# bytes.
data/DATA_DICTIONARY.md text eol=lf
